1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,965
Lesson 3.8.

2
00:00:02,345 --> 00:00:05,766
Thermodynamic length and
energy–time trade-offs.

3
00:00:06,883 --> 00:00:11,200
In the previous lesson we saw that
loading a problem slowly dissipates less.

4
00:00:11,580 --> 00:00:13,947
But with a fixed time there
are infinitely many ways

5
00:00:13,947 --> 00:00:16,086
to go from the initial
energy to the final one.

6
00:00:16,466 --> 00:00:17,535
Which one dissipates least?

7
00:00:17,915 --> 00:00:22,604
The answer is geometric: the space
of control parameters has a metric,

8
00:00:22,604 --> 00:00:26,690
and the best protocol travels
its geodesic at constant speed.

9
00:00:27,070 --> 00:00:29,764
That metric defines the
thermodynamic length.

10
00:00:30,883 --> 00:00:35,386
Imagine crossing ground with mud
of varying depth in a fixed time.

11
00:00:35,766 --> 00:00:40,305
Each stretch costs more the faster
you cross it and the deeper the mud.

12
00:00:40,685 --> 00:00:44,967
If you go at the same speed the whole
way, you pay a lot in the deep zones.

13
00:00:45,347 --> 00:00:49,783
It is better to go slowly where the
mud is deep and fast where it is firm.

14
00:00:50,163 --> 00:00:53,489
In a physical system, the
mud is friction: how slowly

15
00:00:53,489 --> 00:00:56,375
the system readjusts
when we move the control.

16
00:00:57,483 --> 00:01:03,968
Let λ be the control parameter and
X = −∂_λ V the conjugate force.

17
00:01:04,348 --> 00:01:10,011
Near equilibrium, the
excess work is ⟨W_ex⟩ ≈ ∫ λ̇

18
00:01:10,011 --> 00:01:14,880
ζ(λ) λ̇ dt, quadratic
in the control speed.

19
00:01:15,260 --> 00:01:20,372
The coefficient is the friction
tensor, ζ = β ∫ ⟨δX(t) δX(0)⟩

20
00:01:20,372 --> 00:01:25,149
dt: the variance of the force
times its correlation time.

21
00:01:25,529 --> 00:01:29,210
It is another face of the
fluctuation–dissipation theorem: where

22
00:01:29,210 --> 00:01:33,121
fluctuations are large and slow,
moving the control dissipates more.

23
00:01:34,250 --> 00:01:36,417
The friction tensor is a metric.

24
00:01:36,797 --> 00:01:41,941
With it we define the
thermodynamic length, ℒ =

25
00:01:41,941 --> 00:01:46,537
∫ √ζ |dλ|, which does not depend on speed.

26
00:01:46,917 --> 00:01:53,348
The Cauchy–Schwarz inequality
gives the bound ⟨W_ex⟩ ≥ ℒ²/τ.

27
00:01:53,728 --> 00:02:00,116
Equality is reached when the
thermodynamic speed, √ζ λ̇, is constant.

28
00:02:00,496 --> 00:02:03,891
The optimal protocol goes
slowly where friction is high

29
00:02:03,891 --> 00:02:06,792
and fast where it is low,
just like in the mud.

30
00:02:07,917 --> 00:02:11,479
Let's return to the trap we
stiffen from one to four.

31
00:02:11,859 --> 00:02:14,803
Here X = −x²/2,

32
00:02:15,183 --> 00:02:21,417
and with μ = k_B T = 1 the friction
tensor is ζ(κ) = 1/(4κ³): the soft

33
00:02:21,417 --> 00:02:26,672
trap is slow and fluctuates a
lot, so it is costly to move.

34
00:02:27,052 --> 00:02:34,844
The thermodynamic length is
1/√κᵢ − 1/√κ_f, exactly one half.

35
00:02:35,224 --> 00:02:40,701
The optimal protocol is κ(t) =
(1 − t/2τ)⁻²: it starts slowly

36
00:02:40,701 --> 00:02:45,820
and speeds up at the end, when
the trap is already stiff.

37
00:02:46,950 --> 00:02:50,334
Let's compare with the exact
computation of the particle's variance.

38
00:02:50,714 --> 00:02:54,504
With τ = 4, the linear
ramp dissipates 0.076

39
00:02:54,504 --> 00:02:57,777
k_B T in excess; the optimal protocol,

40
00:02:58,157 --> 00:03:04,256
0.063; and the bound ℒ²/τ is 0.0625.

41
00:03:04,636 --> 00:03:07,461
The optimal protocol almost
saturates it, and the

42
00:03:07,461 --> 00:03:09,941
linear ramp dissipates
twenty percent more.

43
00:03:10,321 --> 00:03:15,872
Doubling the time halves the excess:
that is the energy–time trade-off.

44
00:03:16,983 --> 00:03:21,231
Thermodynamic length guides the
design of annealing schedules,

45
00:03:21,231 --> 00:03:25,273
which must slow down near
critical points, the loading of a

46
00:03:25,273 --> 00:03:29,658
thermodynamic chip's couplings,
and erasure and reset protocols.

47
00:03:30,038 --> 00:03:33,104
In unit nine we will see
that the noise schedule

48
00:03:33,104 --> 00:03:35,722
of a diffusion model plays the same role.

49
00:03:36,850 --> 00:03:40,726
Careful: the bound holds in the
slow, linear-response regime.

50
00:03:41,106 --> 00:03:45,555
Don't confuse thermodynamic length,
which lives in parameter space,

51
00:03:45,555 --> 00:03:49,406
with the Wasserstein distance,
which lives in state space.

52
00:03:49,786 --> 00:03:52,257
The optimal protocol is not linear in the

53
00:03:52,257 --> 00:03:54,788
parameter, but in the thermodynamic speed.

54
00:03:55,168 --> 00:03:59,431
And near a critical point friction
grows, and with it the cost.

55
00:04:00,550 --> 00:04:04,720
In summary: near equilibrium,
the excess work is quadratic in

56
00:04:04,720 --> 00:04:08,685
the control speed, with the
friction tensor as the metric.

57
00:04:09,065 --> 00:04:14,830
The thermodynamic length bounds
the excess, ⟨W_ex⟩ ≥ ℒ²/τ, and the

58
00:04:14,830 --> 00:04:20,944
optimal protocol travels the geodesic
at constant thermodynamic speed.

59
00:04:22,050 --> 00:04:25,943
To practice: derive the friction
tensor of the harmonic trap.

60
00:04:26,323 --> 00:04:30,368
Compute the length and the minimum
excess for another stiffness change.

61
00:04:30,748 --> 00:04:33,042
Write the general optimal protocol.

62
00:04:33,422 --> 00:04:37,932
And explain why annealing must slow
down near the critical temperature.
